-- Astronomy & Space · Foundations — The Great Revolution —
-- "Babylon & Greece: the reasonable geocentric cosmos" (from ASTRO-101 Lecture 6,
-- which OPENS Unit B; L6 is split across babylon-and-greece and ptolemy). Curated,
-- human-reviewed master for
-- astronomy-and-space/foundations/great-revolution/babylon-and-greece @
-- research/advanced/read. Applied by db-migrate after seed.sql; idempotent upsert.
--
-- Original, house-styled rework, and the OPENING of Unit B (the great revolution and
-- the birth of astrophysics). The thesis: the Earth-centered cosmos was not stupidity
-- but GOOD SCIENCE. The central puzzle of RETROGRADE motion (the wandering planets'
-- backward loops); why a motionless central Earth was the REASONABLE conclusion from
-- the evidence (no felt motion, straight-down fall, no detectable parallax, the sky
-- wheeling around us); the Greek achievement of explaining the sky with GEOMETRY
-- (Babylonian data + Greek geometry, the circle dogma); ERATOSTHENES measuring the
-- Earth from a shadow; and ARISTARCHUS's heliocentric road-not-taken, reasonably
-- rejected because its predicted parallax was undetectable.
--
-- VISUALS (engagement pass): embeds a ```retrogradesky explorer (the modern
-- relative-motion answer -- the faster Earth overtaking Mars produces the backward
-- loop; RetrogradeSky.tsx) and an ```eratosthenesshadow explorer (shadow angle ->
-- Earth circumference; EratosthenesShadow.tsx), two interactive ```example practice
-- boxes (stellar-parallax, eratosthenes; WorkedExample.tsx), and {image: ...} markers
-- resolving to real imagery at read time. Body Postgres dollar-quoted (no literal
-- currency and no inline KaTeX, so a zero/even '$' count is expected).

with master as (
