with q as (
  select $astroBG_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is retrograde motion, and what is the modern explanation for it?", "options": ["The planets physically stop and reverse in their orbits once a year", "The periodic BACKWARD (westward) looping of a planet against the fixed stars \u2014 normally it drifts east, but it slows, stops, reverses for weeks, then resumes; the modern explanation is an ILLUSION OF RELATIVE MOTION: the faster inner Earth overtakes a slower outer planet (like a fast car passing a slow one), so our line of sight swings backward and the planet appears to loop", "An optical distortion caused by the Earth's atmosphere bending starlight", "The planets speeding up as they get closer to the Sun each year"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Most of the time a planet like Mars drifts slowly eastward against the fixed stars (prograde). Periodically it slows, stops, and loops westward (retrograde) for weeks before resuming. The modern answer is that retrograde is an illusion of relative motion: both Earth and Mars orbit the Sun, but Earth moves faster on its inner orbit and periodically overtakes Mars \u2014 as it does, the sight-line to Mars swings backward, so Mars appears to loop backward against the distant stars. Crucially, this explanation REQUIRES a moving Earth."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does retrograde motion for an outer planet always occur at opposition?", "options": ["Because the planet is farthest from Earth then and moving slowest", "Because opposition (planet opposite the Sun, rising as the Sun sets) is exactly when the faster Earth, on its inner orbit, passes BETWEEN the Sun and the outer planet \u2014 overtaking it \u2014 which is also when the planet is closest and therefore brightest", "Because the Sun's gravity reverses the planet's motion at that point", "It's a coincidence with no physical cause"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Opposition is when the planet sits opposite the Sun in our sky \u2014 and that is precisely the moment Earth, on its inner track, passes between the Sun and the outer planet, overtaking it. Since retrograde is caused by that overtaking, it must coincide with opposition. It also explains why the planet is brightest then: it is closest to Earth. This tight correlation is a natural consequence of the heliocentric picture \u2014 but in the geocentric model it had to be arranged by hand."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why was the conclusion that the Earth stands motionless at the center a REASONABLE, evidence-based one?", "options": ["It wasn't \u2014 it was pure religious dogma imposed by the Church", "Because every available observation supported it: we feel no motion (no concept of inertia yet); objects fall straight down rather than being 'left behind'; no stellar parallax could be detected; and the sky visibly wheels around us \u2014 a moving Earth seemed to CONTRADICT the evidence", "Because the Greeks had measured the Earth's velocity and found it to be zero", "Because Aristotle simply declared it, and no one questioned authority"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Given the evidence available, geocentrism was the better-supported theory. We feel no motion (the flaw: no concept of inertia); dropped objects land at our feet, not 'left behind' by a moving ground (same flaw); nearby stars showed no yearly parallax shift; and the sky wheels around us daily. A moving Earth seemed to contradict all of this. The model was born as empirical science \u2014 its later entanglement with religious authority came centuries afterward. Understanding this is essential: the revolution had to overthrow not a foolish error but a successful, well-evidenced theory."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The Greeks searched for stellar parallax and found none. What did this correctly imply, and why were they wrong to reject a moving Earth?", "options": ["It proved the Earth was flat; they were wrong because the Earth is round", "The absence of parallax left two options \u2014 the Earth doesn't move, OR the stars are so unimaginably far that parallax is too tiny to see; they reasonably chose the first, but the SECOND is true: the stars really are that remote (the nearest's parallax is ~0.77 arcsec, ~78\u00d7 below naked-eye resolution), so the reasoning was sound but the effect was undetectable without telescopes", "It proved the stars were painted on a nearby sphere; they were right", "There is no such thing as parallax; the Greeks invented a fictional effect"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "An orbiting Earth should make nearby stars shift against distant ones over the year (parallax). Seeing none left two possibilities: the Earth doesn't move, or the stars are so far away that the parallax is too small to detect. The Greeks reasonably declined to postulate an absurdly vast, near-empty universe and concluded the Earth stands still. But the second option is the true one \u2014 even the nearest star's parallax is under one arcsecond, ~78 times below what the naked eye can resolve. The logic was sound; only the instruments were lacking. Parallax was finally measured in 1838."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How did Eratosthenes measure the size of the Earth around 240 BC?", "options": ["By sailing all the way around it and measuring the distance travelled", "By noting that at noon on the solstice the Sun was directly overhead at Syene (no shadow) but ~7.2\u00b0 from vertical at Alexandria to the north; since the Sun's rays are parallel, that shadow angle equals the central angle between the cities, and 7.2\u00b0 is 1/50 of a circle \u2014 so the known Alexandria\u2013Syene distance is 1/50 of the whole circumference", "By timing how long it took the Earth's shadow to cross the Moon", "By measuring the curvature of the horizon from a mountaintop"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "At noon on the summer solstice the Sun stood overhead at Syene (a vertical stick cast no shadow), while at the same moment in Alexandria a stick cast a shadow showing the Sun ~7.2\u00b0 from vertical. Because the Sun is so distant its rays arrive parallel, that shadow angle equals the difference in latitude \u2014 the central angle between the cities. Since 7.2\u00b0 is 1/50 of 360\u00b0, the Alexandria\u2013Syene distance is 1/50 of the Earth's circumference; multiplying by 50 gave a result within a few percent of the true 40,000 km. The whole planet, measured with a stick, a shadow, and geometry."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Lay out the case an ancient astronomer had for a motionless central Earth: give four independent pieces of evidence, and for each note the flaw the ancients could not have known. Then explain why understanding this case is essential to understanding the Copernican revolution.", "rubric": "FOUR PIECES OF EVIDENCE (each with its flaw): (1) WE FEEL NO MOTION \u2014 the ground feels perfectly still; if the Earth spun (~1,600 km/h at the equator) and orbited (~100,000 km/h) we should feel it. FLAW: no concept of INERTIA \u2014 we move WITH the Earth and so feel nothing (not understood until Galileo/Newton). (2) THINGS FALL STRAIGHT DOWN \u2014 a dropped stone lands at our feet, not 'left behind' by a moving ground. FLAW: again inertia \u2014 the stone shares the Earth's motion. (3) NO STELLAR PARALLAX \u2014 an orbiting Earth should make nearby stars shift against distant ones over a year; none was seen. FLAW: the stars are so far away that the parallax is far too small to detect without telescopes (nearest ~0.77 arcsec, ~78\u00d7 below naked-eye resolution). (4) THE SKY TURNS AROUND US \u2014 everything wheels around a central Earth daily. FLAW: the same appearance is produced equally by the Earth's OWN SPIN; the turning sky doesn't uniquely require a central Earth. THE VERDICT: given the available evidence, geocentrism was the BETTER-SUPPORTED theory \u2014 a moving Earth seemed to CONTRADICT the evidence at every turn. It was good empirical science, not dogma (the religious entanglement came centuries later). WHY IT MATTERS FOR THE REVOLUTION: only by seeing how reasonable and well-evidenced geocentrism was can we grasp what the Copernican revolution actually required \u2014 NOT the correction of an obvious error, but the OVERTHROW OF A SUCCESSFUL, WELL-EVIDENCED, REASONABLE THEORY, and the explaining-away of all four arguments. That is a far harder, rarer, more remarkable thing, and it teaches how scientific revolutions really work: good theories replaced by better ones, often against the apparent weight of evidence. A strong answer stresses that dismissing the ancients as fools is both historically false and makes the real story impossible to understand."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Describe the Greek achievement of explaining the heavens with geometry \u2014 the two traditions it drew on, the 'circle dogma' and where it came from, and the sophistication shown by Eratosthenes. Then explain the episode of Aristarchus: what he proposed, why it was rejected, and why that rejection was reasonable.", "rubric": "THE GEOMETRIC ACHIEVEMENT: the Greek innovation was the idea that the cosmos could be explained by GEOMETRY \u2014 precise mathematical models \u2014 rather than myth or divine caprice; arguably where mathematical physical science begins. TWO TRADITIONS: (1) the BABYLONIANS kept centuries of meticulous observational records and developed ARITHMETIC methods (number sequences) to PREDICT celestial positions \u2014 computation without a physical picture of why. (2) The GREEKS added GEOMETRY \u2014 building physical models of circles and spheres to EXPLAIN the motions and reveal the cosmos's structure. Babylonian data grounded Greek geometry; Greek geometry structured Babylonian numbers. THE CIRCLE DOGMA: the assumption that all celestial motion must be UNIFORM CIRCULAR MOTION (perfect circles, constant speed), flowing from the Platonic-Aristotelian philosophy that the heavens are a realm of eternal PERFECTION, unlike the corruptible Earth, and only the perfect, endless circle befits them. It both DROVE ancient astronomy (a clear geometric program) and CONSTRAINED it (real orbits are ellipses, so forcing them into circles required ever-more machinery); its overthrow by Kepler was pivotal. ERATOSTHENES: ~240 BC he measured the SIZE OF THE SPHERICAL EARTH to within a few percent using only a stick, a shadow, and geometry \u2014 the Sun overhead at Syene, ~7.2\u00b0 from vertical at Alexandria, that angle = 1/50 of a circle, so the intercity distance = 1/50 of the circumference. The same civilization that centered the cosmos on Earth measured its size with a shadow \u2014 dispelling any notion the ancients were unsophisticated. ARISTARCHUS: ~270 BC he proposed a fully HELIOCENTRIC cosmos \u2014 Sun at the center, Earth and planets orbiting, Earth spinning daily \u2014 reasoning that since the Sun is far larger than the Earth, the small should orbit the great. Essentially the Copernican system, eighteen centuries early. WHY REJECTED, AND REASONABLY: his moving Earth PREDICTS annual stellar PARALLAX; the Greeks looked and found none. This was a real, fair empirical test, and heliocentrism appeared to FAIL it. The escape \u2014 that the stars are so staggeringly distant the parallax is undetectable \u2014 required believing in an almost unimaginable, near-empty universe, which most reasonably declined. So the CORRECT theory was rejected because its real prediction was too small to detect with the instruments of the age (parallax was finally measured in 1838). A strong answer draws the humbling lesson: being RIGHT is not enough \u2014 a theory must be DEMONSTRABLY right, and sometimes the demonstration must wait centuries for the tools."}]$astroBG_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
